NAME
    preview — Display the beginning of a text file with a paginated view.

SYNOPSIS
    preview <file> [lines]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'preview' command displays the contents of a text file a few lines
    at a time. It uses a pager so large files can be read without flooding
    the terminal.

    By default, 25 lines are displayed at a time. You can specify a different
    number of lines per page.

ARGUMENTS
    file    The text file to preview.

    lines   The number of lines displayed per page.
            Defaults to 25.

USAGE
    > preview file.txt
        Displays file.txt 25 lines at a time.

    > preview file.txt 10
        Displays file.txt 10 lines at a time.

PAGER CONTROLS
    Enter       Display 1 more line.
    Space       Display 25 more lines.
    end         Display the entire file.
    q           Quit the preview.

EXAMPLES
    > preview README.txt
        Opens README.txt with 25 lines displayed at a time.

    > preview log.txt 10
        Opens log.txt with 10 lines displayed at a time.

FUN FACTS
    - Large files no longer need to attack your terminal all at once.
    - Space is basically a fast-forward button for reading.
    - q lets you escape before reaching the end.
    - The pager does not add its commands to your CLI history.

